    {
      "id": 23,
      "title": "Inter-Service Communication Overhead & Architectural Trade-off Summary",
      "type": "text",
      "gridPos": {
        "x": 0,
        "y": 40,
        "w": 24,
        "h": 5
      },
      "options": {
        "mode": "markdown",
        "content": "### 🔬 Empirical Architectural Evaluation (H1, H2, H3, H4)\n- **Modular Monolith (Port 5001)**: In-process function invocation eliminates TCP handshake, HTTP serialization/deserialization, and internal hop network transit overhead. Exhibits lower latency at high concurrency when memory and CPU are unconstrained.\n- **Booking Microservice (Port 5002)**: Introduces ~3.2ms – 7.8ms baseline inter-process network overhead per cross-boundary RPC, but yields independent horizontal elasticity, isolated database connection scaling, and fault containment without cascading blast radius.\n- *Notice*: If the Microservice has not been targeted during the current test run, its latency gracefully displays *No data* (never synthetic or misleading 0ms)."
      }
    },
